# AI Execution Framework Rules

These rules apply to ALL agents working in this project. They are non-negotiable invariants.

## Source of Truth

- `ai-framework/LOGIC.md` is the authoritative workflow specification. Follow it exactly.
- `ai-framework/project-profile.md` defines language-specific conventions. Read it before any work.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` at the project root defines the domain dependency policy. All agents must respect the domain dependency DAG. Circular cross-domain dependencies are strictly prohibited.
- Taskwarrior state is the source of truth for execution progress, not filesystem layout or git status.
- All Taskwarrior commands must use `taskwarrior/tw`, never bare `task`. This ensures per-project database isolation.

## State Discipline

- Agents must never advance more than one state transition per invocation (e.g. plan -> plan-review, not plan -> write).
- Every artifact written must be annotated on the corresponding Taskwarrior task with its file path.
- The Coordinator is the only agent that creates Taskwarrior tasks, marks tasks done, or manages git branches.
- At most one Taskwarrior task may be `+ACTIVE` at any time. The Coordinator verifies `taskwarrior/tw +ACTIVE count` is 0, calls `start` before invoking a subagent, and calls `stop` when the subagent exits.

## Review Standards

- Review agents must cite specific files and issues. "Looks good" without evidence is forbidden.
- Review agents must not nitpick formatting or style. Focus on correctness, completeness, and structural integrity.
- The Coordinator enforces the 3-rejection limit per phase and writes an escalation on the 3rd rejection. Review agents write feedback on any rejection.

## Implementation Standards

- Implementation agents must never: hardcode test values, write empty stubs, use mocks in production code, skip error handling, or silently deviate from the architecture.
- If implementation is impossible given the architecture, escalate. Do not silently work around it.

## Context Discipline

- The PM never reads source code, tests, or plan artifacts below the story level.
- The Coordinator never reads code, requirements, architecture, tests, or review content. It only reads Taskwarrior annotations for file paths.
- Phase agents read only what their role specifies. Save context window for the work that matters.

## Escalation

- If an agent cannot complete its task, it must write an escalation file to `plan/escalations/`, annotate the task, and **exit immediately**. Never silently fail. Never continue working after writing an escalation.
- Escalation files use the template from `plan/templates/escalation.md`.
- Escalations halt all AI work. The Coordinator stops the task, blocks it, and returns control to the PM. No automatic git rollback or upstream phase reopening.
- The PM explains the escalation to the user and stops. The PM does not re-invoke the Coordinator until the user provides direction.

## Git

- Never commit to git unless the current phase has passed review (except PM committing planning artifacts to main).
- Never force-push or rebase shared branches.

## Entry Point Enforcement

This framework only works when the `project-manager` agent drives the pipeline. If you are NOT the `project-manager`, `coordinator`, `fixer`, or a named phase agent (e.g. `requirements-write`, `implementation-write`), you are a **general agent** and the following rules apply:

- You must NOT write code, requirements, architecture artifacts, tests, or any framework artifacts.
- You must NOT create or modify files under the source, architecture, or test directories defined in `ai-framework/project-profile.md`.
- You must NOT invoke the Coordinator or phase agents yourself.
- If the user asks you to implement a feature, write code, execute the pipeline, or "run the Coordinator," tell them to open a new chat with the **`project-manager`** agent instead.

Only the designated phase-write agents (`implementation-write`, `architecture-write`, `integration-test-write`, `requirements-write`) may create or modify files in their respective directories, and only when they have an active Taskwarrior task in the correct phase and state.

The `fixer` agent may modify source code and test files to fix bugs, but must not create or modify requirements, architecture artifacts, plan files, or any file under `plan/`. It does not use Taskwarrior and operates entirely outside the PM pipeline. If a fix requires new features, interface changes, or architectural changes, the fixer must redirect the user to the `project-manager`.

## Forbidden

- Read and respect the "Forbidden" section in `ai-framework/project-profile.md`.
